# The Nodes list's filter chips: which of the roster is below them, as a control the reader can
# see rather than a keycap they have to be told about.
#
# Four things to watch, and the last is the one that would be a bug nobody could see.
#
#   - The strip is a *row of the list*, so the cursor stops on it and the highlight is the same
#     rectangle every other row gets. It is not the navigation bar three rows above it, which is
#     a recessed tier because it is chrome around the body; this stands in the body because it
#     is content about what the rows below it are. It is one step where every other row here is
#     two, which is the variable-height list model doing what it landed for.
#   - The chosen chip wears a check and a tonal fill - Material's filter chip, and the two say
#     the same thing twice so a reader scanning shape and a reader reading words both get it.
#   - The heading's count is what is *drawn*, not what is held. "Nodes (n of m)" already means
#     "there is more than this", so a filter needs no sentence of its own; watch the first
#     number fall as the chips step and the second stay where it is.
#   - Under Direct, every row has a staircase against its trailing edge and none of them says
#     "hop" or "mqtt". That is the chip asking mesh_ui_node_signal_heard() rather than a looser
#     question of its own - if it asked `hops_away == 0` instead, Delta Camp would be in this
#     list with nothing beside its name.

scene demo
scale 4
clock 2026-01-12 19:12
config

# One node the reader has chosen to keep, so the last chip has something to show.
pin ECHO

tab nodes
hold 1600

# All -> Direct. The nodes heard with no relay in the way, which is the question the trailing
# staircase answers one row at a time and this answers for the whole list.
key a
hold 2200

# The rows under it are still rows: the cursor walks off the strip into them and A opens a node
# exactly as it does with no filter on. This is the half that is a picture of a bug if it is
# wrong - a filtered row that opened the roster's node rather than the row's would look right.
key down 4
hold 1200
key a
hold 1800
key b
hold 700

# Direct -> Pinned. The star in the marker gutter is the same fact this chip filters on, which
# is why the chip is called what X is called.
key up 4
key a
hold 2200

# And back round to All, which is where the wrap makes three chips enough without a second key.
key a
hold 2000
